content.text:
Done. Truncation at the last word boundary, with a hard-cut fallback.

**`format.js`** — one block appended after `suffix`:

```js
if (options.truncate && result.length > options.truncate) {
  const max = options.truncate;
  const head = result.slice(0, Math.max(0, max - 3));
  const lastSpace = head.lastIndexOf(' ');
  const kept = lastSpace > 0 ? head.slice(0, lastSpace) : head;
  result = (kept.trimEnd() + '...').slice(0, max);
}
```

`Math.max(0, max - 3)` matters more than it looks: a bare `max - 3` goes negative when `maxLength < 3`, and `slice(0, -1)` counts from the *end* in JS, which would silently return most of the string instead of truncating it.

**`format.test.js`** — six assertions in the existing `console.assert` style: short-string passthrough, exact-length passthrough, word-boundary cut, long-token fallback, interaction with `suffix`, and `maxLength: 2`.

TDD cycle: wrote the tests first, ran them, watched four fail with no truncate branch present, then implemented. All pass now. Two of the six (the passthrough cases) passed before the implementation existed, since passthrough was already the behavior — they guard against the new branch over-truncating rather than proving new behavior.

Two things I left alone, both pre-existing and outside what you approved:

- `node format.test.js` emits a `MODULE_TYPELESS_PACKAGE_JSON` warning. `package.json` has no `"type": "module"` while the files use ESM. One line to fix, but it's unrelated to truncation.
- `console.assert` doesn't halt or set an exit code, so `"All tests passed"` prints even when assertions fail — it did during the RED run above. Worth knowing if anything ever scripts this.

